                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Painted siding and trim joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100% silicone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glass, metal, unpainted joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane or hybrid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent or as labeled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vinyl, fiber cement, masonry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tripolymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, after cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mineral spirits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Metal, roofing, damp masonry</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Butyl rubber</strong> is the fifth family and the one most people should skip for visible joints. It stays tacky, collects dirt and takes a very long time to firm up, though it still has a place on gutter seams and metal roof laps. If you only remember one rule from this section, make it this: if the joint will be painted, choose elastomeric latex or a paintable polymer; if it will never be painted and sees heavy water, choose silicone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Reading the Label: Movement Class and ASTM Standards
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful number on a sealant label is its movement rating, and the most reliable way to find it is the ASTM standard printed on the back. Two standards matter for homeowners. <strong>ASTM C834</strong> covers latex sealants and is a fairly low bar; a product that only claims C834 is essentially a painter's caulk. <strong>ASTM C920</strong> covers elastomeric joint sealants and comes with a class number that tells you how much the joint can move.
          </p>

          <p>
            A C920 Class 25 sealant is designed to handle plus or minus 25 percent of the joint's width. Class 35 and Class 50 products handle more. Some labels go further and add letters for grade (NS means non-sag, so it stays put in a vertical joint) and use (exposure to traffic, immersion, or specific substrates). You do not need to memorize the codes, but a tube that carries a C920 class number is making a testable claim, and that is worth more than any adjective on the front.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What the Movement Classes Mean</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±25%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 25</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">±35%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 35</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ASTM C920 Class 50</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Low</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical ASTM C834 latex</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Translate that into a real joint. A 3/8-inch gap between two wood boards on a sunny wall might open and close by a sixteenth of an inch or more over a year. That is roughly 17 percent of the width, which a Class 25 sealant handles and a plain latex caulk does not. Wider joints are more forgiving because the same movement is a smaller percentage, which is one reason sealant makers discourage joints narrower than about a quarter inch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond movement, scan the label for three more things: the minimum application temperature, the time before rain exposure, and the time before paint. Those three numbers decide whether you can do the job this weekend, and they vary more between products than most people expect. If the tube is vague about any of them, look up the manufacturer's technical data sheet, which almost always spells them out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Match the Caulk to the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Adhesion is the other half of the decision. A sealant that stretches beautifully is useless if it peels off one side of the joint, and different families grab different materials. Before you buy, identify both sides of every joint you plan to seal, because the harder of the two decides the product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood siding and trim</h3>

          <p>
            Painted wood is the easiest substrate. Elastomeric latex like Dynaflex 230 or Big Stretch bonds well, paints well and handles seasonal movement. Avoid silicone on wood you intend to paint, and never use caulk to hide soft or rotted wood; dig the rot out and rebuild it with an epoxy product from our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">wood filler guide</a> before you seal the joint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vinyl, fiber cement and composite</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl expands and contracts a lot with temperature and has a slick surface that latex struggles to hold. A polymer sealant such as OSI Quad is the safer pick, especially in a color matched to the siding. Fiber cement makers publish their own sealant requirements in their installation instructions, typically calling for a high-quality, paintable elastomeric or polymer sealant meeting ASTM C920, so check those before buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brick, stone and stucco</h3>

          <p>
            Masonry is porous, alkaline and often a little dusty. Polymer and tripolymer sealants such as Geocel 2300 grip it far better than latex, and they tolerate the slight dampness masonry tends to hold. Wire-brush the edges first. For cracks in the masonry itself rather than joints between materials, a dedicated masonry sealant is the better tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glass, aluminum and metal flashing</h3>

          <p>
            Nonporous surfaces are where silicone shines. For glass-to-frame joints, aluminum window flanges and metal flashing that will not be painted, use 100 percent silicone. If the metal will be painted, switch to a paintable polymer instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Climate and Application Temperature
          </h2>

          <p>
            Your local weather changes the ranking. In cold climates the sealant must stay flexible at low temperatures, because a joint is at its widest when the materials around it have shrunk in winter. That is precisely when a stiff caulk tears. Elastomeric latex and polymer sealants keep their stretch in the cold; cheap acrylic does not, which is why winter is when painter's caulk outdoors fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            In hot, high-sun climates the enemy is UV. Ultraviolet light slowly breaks down the binders in latex products, leaving a chalky, cracked surface on south and west walls within a few years. Silicone is the most UV-stable choice for unpainted joints, and a good coat of exterior paint over an elastomeric latex sealant protects it from the sun and extends its life considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Application temperature is a separate issue from service temperature. Most water-based caulks list a minimum application temperature around 40 degrees Fahrenheit, and that limit applies to the surface as well as the air. A north wall at dawn in October can be several degrees colder than the forecast high. Polymer and solvent-based sealants usually tolerate lower temperatures; some are labeled for application near freezing or below. Read the tube for the exact number.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TIMING RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Caulk in the shoulder seasons, mid-morning to mid-afternoon.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ideal conditions are dry surfaces, temperatures between roughly 50 and 80 degrees, and no rain for at least the window printed on the tube. Joints sealed at a mid-range temperature sit at a mid-range width, so the bead stretches and compresses evenly as seasons change.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Avoid caulking in full afternoon sun on a dark wall in summer. The joint is at its narrowest then, so the bead will be stretched hard all winter, and the hot surface skins the caulk before you can tool it. If you have no choice, work the shaded side of the house and follow the shade around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joint-design" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Joint Size, Depth and Backer Rod
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealant's movement rating only applies if the bead has the right shape. The ideal joint is wider than it is deep, bonded to the two sides of the gap and not to the back. That hourglass profile lets the sealant stretch like a rubber band. A bead that is too deep, or glued to the bottom of the joint, cannot stretch evenly and tears at the edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            The working rule from sealant manufacturers is a depth of about half the width, with a minimum depth around a quarter inch and a typical maximum width around half to three-quarters of an inch for gun-grade products. A half-inch-wide joint wants about a quarter inch of sealant depth. Anything deeper than that should be filled with closed-cell foam backer rod first.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Joint width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Target sealant depth</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Backer rod size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually none</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minimum practical width</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/2 inch rod if deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common trim and siding gap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5/8 inch rod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rod about 25% wider than gap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 inch rod</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near the upper limit for most tubes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 1 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not recommended</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rebuild the joint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix framing or add trim first</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Backer rod does three jobs. It sets the depth of the bead, it gives you something to tool against, and it prevents three-sided adhesion, where the sealant bonds to the bottom of the joint as well as the sides. Choose a rod roughly 25 percent wider than the gap so it stays put by compression, push it in with a blunt tool, and do not puncture closed-cell rod, which can off-gas and blister the sealant above it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For shallow joints where rod will not fit, such as the gap where lap siding meets a corner board, a strip of bond-breaker tape at the bottom of the joint does the same job. And if a gap is more than about an inch wide, caulk is the wrong fix entirely. Add trim, rebuild the framing or install flashing, then seal the narrower joint that remains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Laying a Bead That Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Good sealant applied badly will fail as fast as bad sealant. The process is short, but each step matters. Start by removing the old bead completely. Cut it along both edges with a sharp knife or a hooked removal tool, pull it out, and scrape the residue. New caulk over old caulk bonds only to the old bead, which is already failing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the joint next. Brush out dust and paint flakes, wipe the edges with a rag dampened with isopropyl alcohol, and let everything dry. Mildew should be killed with a diluted bleach solution, rinsed and dried. If the joint was silicone before, scrub the residue hard with a silicone remover, because almost nothing sticks to a silicone film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle at about 45 degrees with an opening slightly smaller than the joint, and push the bead ahead of the nozzle rather than dragging it behind. Pushing forces sealant into the gap; pulling lays it on top. Keep steady pressure and move at a pace that fills the joint fully without heaping. A dripless gun with a smooth thrust ratio makes this far easier than a bargain-bin gun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tool the bead within a few minutes, before it skins. A plastic tooling spoon or a wet finger presses the sealant against both sides and leaves a slightly concave surface that sheds water. Painter's tape on each side of a visible joint gives a crisp line; pull it right after tooling, before the bead skins over. Our <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">step-by-step window caulking guide</a> walks through the same process on the joint homeowners seal most often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in sections no longer than you can tool in a few minutes, especially with fast-skinning silicone and polymers. A three-foot run is plenty in warm weather. Keep a damp rag for latex or the right solvent for polymers within reach, and wipe smears immediately; cured polymer sealant on siding is very hard to remove cleanly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Curing, Rain Windows and Painting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caulk dries from the outside in. The surface skins over in minutes to an hour, which is the point at which light rain or dust will not mar it, but the inside stays soft for much longer. Full cure for a latex bead of normal size takes several days, and a thick polymer or silicone bead can take a week or more. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">how long caulk takes to dry</a> breaks the timing down by chemistry and conditions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain is the real deadline. Water-based caulk that gets soaked before it skins can wash out of the joint or cure with a weak, chalky surface. Most labels print a rain-ready time, from under an hour for some silicones to several hours for latex. Check the forecast for that window plus a margin, and remember that dew counts as moisture on cool evenings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Painting is where chemistry matters again. Latex and elastomeric latex caulks typically accept paint within an hour or two to a day, depending on the product and conditions. Polymer sealants often need longer before paint, and some recommend waiting until the bead has firmed up. Silicone cannot be painted at all. Painting too soon can cause the paint to crack as the bead underneath continues to move and shrink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a quality exterior acrylic paint over sealant, not an oil-based paint, which is stiffer and tends to crack over a flexible bead. Paint protects latex sealant from UV and is one of the cheapest ways to add years to a caulk job. A topcoat that extends a few millimeters onto each side of the joint also seals the bond line, which is where water tries to sneak in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cost, Coverage and How Many Tubes to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior sealant is cheap relative to the damage it prevents, so this is not the place to save a few dollars per tube. At the time of writing, basic acrylic caulk runs roughly $3 to $6 per 10.1 oz tube, elastomeric latex roughly $6 to $12, and polymer, silicone and tripolymer sealants roughly $8 to $15. A whole house rarely needs more than 10 to 20 tubes, so the gap between the cheapest and the best is usually under $100.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage depends almost entirely on bead size. A standard 10.1 oz cartridge holds a little over 18 cubic inches of sealant. Fill a square quarter-inch joint and that is roughly 24 linear feet; at an eighth of an inch it stretches toward 90 feet. A triangular fillet bead on an outside corner uses less material per foot than a full-depth joint, which is why tube labels often quote higher figures than the math for a filled gap.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Bead size (width x depth)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. feet per 10.1 oz tube</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical job</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/8 x 1/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 90 feet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hairline trim gaps</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/4 x 1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 24 feet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Window and door perimeters</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/8 x 1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 16 feet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Siding to trim</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/2 x 1/4 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 12 feet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide trim joints with backer rod</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            To estimate a whole job, measure the perimeter of each window and door, add corner boards, butt joints and penetrations, and divide by the coverage for your typical bead. Then add 20 percent for waste, tooling and the tube that inevitably cures in the nozzle overnight. A standard double-hung window perimeter is about 18 to 20 feet, so one tube per window is a realistic planning number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where to spend: buy the premium sealant for the joints that face weather and move, and use a budget acrylic only for small, protected, painted gaps. Do not buy the bulk 28 oz cartridges unless you have a large, continuous job; once opened, a cartridge cures within days, and the waste wipes out the savings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Inspection and When to Recaulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even the best exterior sealant is a maintenance item. Plan on a quick walk around the house each spring and fall, looking at window and door perimeters, trim joints, penetrations for pipes and wires, and the joints where different materials meet. The goal is to catch failing beads before water has a path into the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recaulk when you see any of four signs: the bead has pulled away from one side of the joint, it has cracked through, it has turned hard and brittle, or it has shrunk so far that the joint shows daylight. Surface crazing on a painted bead is often cosmetic, so press it with a fingernail; if the sealant underneath is still rubbery and bonded on both sides, repainting may be all it needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Typical service lives vary by product and exposure. Plain acrylic caulk on a sunny wall may last only a few years; a good elastomeric latex under paint commonly lasts much longer, and silicone and polymer sealants are often rated for decades on the label. Manufacturer durability claims assume correct joint design and surface prep, so a bead laid over old caulk or without backer rod will not reach them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealant is one part of a larger water-management system. Make sure gutters are flowing so water does not sheet down the siding and overload the joints, which is where a set of <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">gutter guards</a> can help, and keep wood surfaces protected with a good finish such as the options in our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a>. Caulk keeps water out of the gaps; the rest of the envelope should keep most of it off the walls to begin with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using painter's caulk on siding joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cheap acrylic caulk labeled only to ASTM C834 has very little stretch. On butt joints, corner boards and sunny walls it cracks within a season or two because the joint moves more than the bead can follow. Use an elastomeric latex or a polymer sealant carrying an ASTM C920 movement class on any joint that works.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking over the old bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh caulk laid over a failing bead bonds only to the old material, which is already letting go. Within months the whole sandwich peels off together. Cut out the old sealant completely, scrape the residue, clean the edges, and only then lay the new bead against bare, sound surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling deep gaps with sealant alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bead that is deeper than it is wide cannot stretch evenly, shrinks as it cures and wastes several tubes. It also tends to bond to the back of the joint. Push in closed-cell backer rod sized about a quarter larger than the gap so the sealant depth is roughly half the joint width.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting silicone where paint is planned</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint beads up and peels on silicone, and the residue keeps contaminating the joint long after you cut the bead out. If a joint will ever be painted, use a paintable elastomeric or polymer sealant from the start. Save silicone for glass, metal and joints you are certain will stay unpainted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing weep holes and drainage gaps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some gaps are meant to stay open. Window sill weep slots, the bottom edge of window nailing flanges in some installations, brick weep holes and the bottom of lap siding courses let trapped water drain out. Caulking them shut turns a minor leak into rot inside the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which caulk should most homeowners buy for outdoor joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most painted homes, an elastomeric latex sealant such as DAP Dynaflex 230 is the best all-around choice. It cleans up with water, accepts paint, bonds to the common siding and trim materials and handles far more joint movement than ordinary acrylic caulk. Switch to a polymer for vinyl and masonry, or silicone for glass and metal that will never be painted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silicone or latex better for exterior joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether the joint will be painted. Silicone is more waterproof and UV-stable, but paint will not stick to it and it grips porous wood and masonry poorly. Elastomeric latex is paintable, easy to work with and flexible enough for most siding and trim. For painted wood, choose latex. For glass, aluminum and unpainted metal, choose silicone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What service life can I expect from an outdoor sealant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plain acrylic caulk on a sunny wall can fail within a few years. A quality elastomeric latex protected by paint commonly lasts much longer, and many silicone and polymer sealants carry multi-decade durability claims on the label. Those claims assume proper joint design, backer rod where needed and a bead laid on clean, bare surfaces, so preparation matters as much as the product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I caulk exterior joints in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based caulks list a minimum application temperature around 40 degrees Fahrenheit for both air and surface, and they must stay above freezing while they skin over. Some polymer and solvent-based sealants are labeled for colder conditions. Check the tube, measure the surface temperature on shaded walls, and avoid caulking when frost or dew is likely before the bead skins.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What caulk works on vinyl siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl moves a lot with temperature and has a slick surface, so a polymer sealant such as OSI Quad is the usual choice. It grips vinyl well, stays flexible and comes in colors matched to common siding shades. Do not caulk the overlaps where vinyl panels lock together; they are designed to slide and to drain, and sealing them causes buckling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use caulk on brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a polymer or tripolymer sealant, not acrylic, where brick meets wood trim, windows or doors. Products such as Geocel 2300 grip porous, slightly damp masonry well and handle the movement between dissimilar materials. Wire-brush loose material from the edges first. Never seal brick weep holes, and repair cracked mortar joints with mortar rather than caulk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need backer rod for every joint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Joints a quarter inch wide or narrower and shallow joints usually need no rod. Any joint deeper than about half its width should get closed-cell foam backer rod so the sealant ends up about half as deep as it is wide. Where rod will not fit, bond-breaker tape at the bottom of the joint prevents three-sided adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
